"LSWH100: A handshape dataset for Brazilian sign language (Libras) using SignWriting - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11357844/)\nciteturn33view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11357844/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 448\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Search\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Log in 【3†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【4†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL5: \nL6:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL7:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL8:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL9:   * [Button: Log out ]\nL10: \nL11: Search… [Input: Search NCBI] Search NCBI\nL12: \nL13: Primary site navigation\nL14: \nL15: 【8†Image: Close†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL16: \nL17: Search [Input: Search NCBI]\nL18: \nL19: Logged in as:\nL20:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL21:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL22:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL23: \nL24: [Button: Log in ]\nL25: \nL26: 【1†Image: PMC search open icon†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【8†Image: PMC search close ison†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL27: \nL28: Search PMC Full-Text Archive [Input: Search PMC Full-Text Archive] Search in PMC 【9†Image: Search†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL29: \nL30:   * 【10†Journal List】\nL31:   * 【11†User Guide】\nL32: \nL33: [Button: Data Brief]\nL34: \nL35: . 2024 Jul 31;56:110780. doi: 【12†10.1016/j.dib.2024.110780†doi.org】\nL36: # LSWH100: A handshape dataset for Brazilian sign language (Libras) using SignWriting\nL37: \nL38: 【13†Vicente Coelho Lobo-Neto†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL39: \nL40: ### Vicente Coelho Lobo-Neto\nL41: \nL42: ^{1}Institute of Computing, University of Campinas, Av. Albert Einstein 1251, Campinas, SP 13083-852, Brazil\nL43: \nL44: Find articles by 【13†Vicente Coelho Lobo-Neto†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL45: \nL46: ^{1}, 【14†Helio Pedrini†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL47: ### Helio Pedrini\nL48: \nL49: ^{1}Institute of Computing, University of Campinas, Av. Albert Einstein 1251, Campinas, SP 13083-852, Brazil\nL50: \nL51: Find articles by 【14†Helio Pedrini†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL52: \nL53: ^{1,}^{⁎}\nL54: \nL55:   * [Button: Author information]\nL56:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL57:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL58: \nL59: ^{1}Institute of Computing, University of Campinas, Av. Albert Einstein 1251, Campinas, SP 13083-852, Brazil\nL60: \nL61: ^{⁎}\nL62: \nL63: Corresponding author. helio@ic.unicamp.br\nL64: Received 2024 Jun 3; Revised 2024 Jul 23; Accepted 2024 Jul 24; Collection date 2024 Oct.\nL65: \nL66: © 2024 The Author(s)\nL67: \nL68: This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).\nL69: \nL70: 【15†PMC Copyright notice】\nL71: \nL72: PMCID: PMC11357844  PMID: 【16†39211486†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL73: ## Abstract\nL74: This paper presents Libras SignWriting Handshape (LSWH100), a new handshape dataset focused on Sign Language Recognition. The dataset includes 144,000 synthetic images of a realistic human hand, covering 100 distinct handshape classes used in Brazilian Sign Language (Libras). Handshapes are named using the convention from SignWriting, a writing system for sign languages. The dataset contains annotations for classification, detection, segmentation, depth estimation, and 3D hand keypoints.\nL75: Images include indoor and outdoor scenes during different times of day, centered on a single hand that can change size, 3D rotation, and skin tone. We generated these images using Blender, a free and open-source 3D creation software. This is a challenging dataset that can be further explored. With a focus on sign language, this dataset has the potential to advance sign language recognition systems, positively impacting those who rely on sign language for communication.\nL76: Keywords: Computer vision, Handshape recognition, Hand configuration recognition, Hand keypoints\nL77: \nL78: * * *\nL79: \nL80: Specifications Table\nL81: Subject  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL82: Specific subject area  | Handshape recognition and detection using hand images, classes, bounding boxes, segmentation masks and/or keypoints\nL83: Type of data  | Image\nL84: Raw\nL85: Data collection  | The data was generated synthetically. To generate the data, we chose the software Blender.\nL86: Data source location  | Synthetically generated using computational resources from the Institute of Computing of the University of Campinas - Av. Albert Einstein 1251, Campinas-SP, Brazil, 13083-852\nL87: Data accessibility  | Repository name: Zenodo\nL88: Data identification number: 【17†https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10635628†doi.org】\nL89: Direct URL to data: 【18†https://zenodo.org/records/10635628†zenodo.org】\nL90: Related research article  | None\nL91: 【19†Open in a new tab】\nL92: ## 1. Value of the Data\nL93: \nL94:   * •\nL95: \nL96: Our dataset includes 100 handshapes used in the Brazilian Sign Language (Libras). Each handshape is depicted from four perspectives: front, back, left, and right.\nL97: \nL98:   * •\nL99: The handshapes were selected following the convention defined by the SignWriting system. Created by Valerie Sutton and formally described by Steve Slevinski, it is a logographic system designed for writing sign language words. It employs a collection of symbols to represent handshapes, hand movements, and facial expressions.\nL100: \nL101:   * •\nL102: The images include changes in the basic properties of the hand (size, rotation, and position within the image), diverse backgrounds (indoor and outdoor), and various lighting conditions (morning, afternoon, and night).\nL103: \nL104:   * •\nL105: \nL106: The dataset consists of 48,000 RGB images, divided into three sets: training, validation, and testing. Additionally to RGB, each perspective includes segmentation masks and depth images, resulting in a total of 144,000 images.\nL107: \nL108:   * •\nL109: The annotations use the COCO format, which is widely used and encompasses the image class and coordinates for bounding boxes, segmentation masks, and hand keypoints.\nL110: \nL111:   * •\nL112: \nL113: Although we selected the handshapes based on how often they are used in Libras, multiple sign languages share the same handshapes, with different meanings and applications. Also, other Human–Computer Interaction (HCI) systems can also use the same handshapes, which increases the dataset usability.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLSWH100 - Libras SignWriting Handshape | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/records/10635628)\nciteturn33view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://zenodo.org/records/10635628\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 185\nL0: Published June 3, 2024 | Version 1\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Dataset】 Open\nL3: \nL4: # LSWH100 - Libras SignWriting Handshape\nL5: \nL6: ### Authors/Creators\nL7: \nL8:   * 【1†Coelho Lobo-Neto, Vicente (Researcher)^{1}】\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Show affiliations ]\nL11: \nL12:   * 1. Universidade Estadual de Campinas (UNICAMP)\nL13: \nL14: ### Contributors\nL15: \nL16: ### Supervisor:\nL17: \nL18:   * 【2†Pedrini, Helio^{1}】\nL19: \nL20: [Button: Show affiliations ]\nL21: \nL22:   * 1. Universidade Estadual de Campinas (UNICAMP)\nL23: ## Description\nL24: Libras SignWriting Handshape (LSWH100) is a new handshape dataset focused on Sign Language Recognition. The dataset includes 144,000 synthetic images of a realistic human hand, covering 100 distinct handshape classes used in Brazillian Sign Language (Libras). Each handshape is represented from four different perspectives: front, back, left and right. However, the perspectives are independent of each other.\nL25: In other words, the hands represented in one perspective will not be the same as those in other perspectives. Handshapes are named using the convention from SignWriting, a writing system for sign languages. The dataset contains annotations for classification, detection, segmentation, depth estimation, and 3D hand keypoints. Images include indoor and outdoor scenes during different times of day, centered on a single hand that can change size, 3D rotation, and skin tone.\nL26: We generated these images using Blender, a free and open-source 3D creation software.\nL27: ## Files\nL28: \nL29: ###\nL30: \nL31: back.zip\nL32: \nL33: 【3†Preview】\nL34: ###\nL35: \nL36: Files (17.7 GB)\nL37: Name  | Size  |\nL38: --- | --- | ---\nL39: 【4†back.zip】 md5:dd64afc5e66ef379bac803283fe9edd7  | 4.4 GB  | 【3†Preview】 【4†Download】\nL40: 【5†class_list.csv】 md5:64a84d39a985136d5b75ef333328a1ce  | 2.8 kB  | 【6†Preview】 【5†Download】\nL41: 【7†class_list_with_images.pdf】 md5:0a827ae24019d34dbe8f515d09a6fb84  | 8.6 MB  | 【8†Preview】 【7†Download】\nL42: 【9†front.zip】 md5:8a7e51f41e43b418b4a36973ce045e14  | 4.5 GB  | 【10†Preview】 【9†Download】\nL43: 【11†left.zip】 md5:2a09cebbdbd239d158f793282a144d86  | 4.3 GB  | 【12†Preview】 【11†Download】\nL44: 【13†right.zip】 md5:a289a32385e46532dc817ab946835915  | 4.4 GB  | 【14†Preview】 【13†Download】\nL45: 【15†test_annotations.coco.json】 md5:5a7a44a526c955b61f9a9bdf4581a8e3  | 11.7 MB  | 【16†Preview】 【15†Download】\nL46: 【17†train_annotations.coco.json】 md5:15211578a754cfa3024a99b7765d283e  | 116.7 MB  | 【18†Preview】 【17†Download】\nL47: 【19†val_annotations.coco.json】 md5:9fe7075360776c27cb6ec3d5aca5297f  | 11.7 MB  | 【20†Preview】 【19†Download】\nL48: ## Additional details\nL49: \nL50: ### Related works\nL51: \nL52: Continues\nL53:     Thesis: 【21†https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.12733/6081†hdl.handle.net】 (URL)\nL54: Is described by\nL55:     Journal article: 【22†10.1016/j.dib.2024.110780†doi.org】 (DOI)\nL56: \nL57: 632\nL58: \nL59: Views\nL60: \nL61: 1K\nL62: \nL63: Downloads\nL64: \nL65: Show more details\nL66: \nL67:  | All versions  | This version\nL68: --- | --- | ---\nL69: Views  | 632  | 632\