ampos†dcc.ufmg.br】^{1}, 【12†Erickson R. Nascimento†dcc.ufmg.br】^{1}\nL8: ^{1}【13†Universidade Federal de Minas Gerais (UFMG)†www.ufmg.br】\nL9: ^{2}【14†Universidade Federal de Viçosa (UFV)†www.ufv.br】\nL10: \nL11: 【15†Read Paper】 【16†View Repo†github.com】 【17†Dataset Part 1†huggingface.co】 【18†Dataset Part 2†huggingface.co】\nL12: \nL13: ⚠️ Important: Our dataset requires credentialized access. The datasets on HuggingFace are gated and access must be authorized after you agree to the terms and conditions. Please visit the dataset repositories and request access through HuggingFace.\nL14: ## Dataset Overview\nL15: \nL16: Captar-Libras is a large-scale multimodal dataset for Brazilian Sign Language (Libras), comprising video sequences from native Deaf signers performing sentences in the medical domain. Data was collected in a controlled capture environment using four synchronized cameras: a frontal RGB-D camera, two wide-angle cameras covering lateral and zenithal views, and a dedicated high-definition facial camera. This enables rich spatial and temporal modeling of body, hand, and facial expressions.\nL17: Each recording follows a guided protocol to ensure linguistic consistency and is annotated with frame-level gloss temporal alignments produced by specialist annotators. The dataset includes raw multi-view recordings, preprocessed video segments, structured gloss and text annotations, and precomputed full-body pose sequences in SMPL-X format. A signer-independent train/validation/test split stratified by gender and skin tone is provided as the recommended evaluation protocol.\nL18: ### Key Statistics\nL19: \nL20: ### Over 24,000\nL21: \nL22: Video Sequences\nL23: \nL24: ### 78\nL25: \nL26: Signers (50F / 28M)\nL27: \nL28: ### 375\nL29: \nL30: Sentences\nL31: \nL32: ### 4\nL33: \nL34: Camera Views\nL35: \nL36: ## Data Access\nL37: \nL38: ### Annotations\nL39: \nL40: Structured gloss and text annotations for all sequences in the dataset.\nL41: \nL42: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL43: \nL44: Copy\nL45: \nL46:     ``\nL47: \nL48: ### Frontal RGB\nL49: \nL50: High-resolution frontal RGB video streams from the frontal camera.\nL51: \nL52: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL53: \nL54: Copy\nL55: \nL56:     ``\nL57: ### Frontal Depth\nL58: \nL59: Depth sequences from the frontal RGB-D camera for 3D reconstruction.\nL60: \nL61: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL62: \nL63: Copy\nL64: \nL65:     ``\nL66: \nL67: ### Lateral View\nL68: \nL69: Wide-angle camera covering lateral perspective (side view).\nL70: \nL71: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL72: \nL73: Copy\nL74: \nL75:     ``\nL76: \nL77: ### Zenithal View\nL78: \nL79: Wide-angle camera covering zenithal perspective (top-down view).\nL80: \nL81: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL82: \nL83: Copy\nL84: \nL85:     ``\nL86: ### Facial HD\nL87: \nL88: Dedicated high-definition facial camera for fine-grained facial expression analysis.\nL89: \nL90: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL91: \nL92: Copy\nL93: \nL94:     ``\nL95: \nL96: ### SMPL-X Poses\nL97: \nL98: Precomputed full-body 3D pose sequences in SMPL-X format for all sequences.\nL99: \nL100: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL101: \nL102: Copy\nL103: \nL104:     ``\nL105: \nL106: ### Preprocessed Videos\nL107: \nL108: Preprocessed video segments used in the baseline experiments.\nL109: \nL110: 【15†Show Download Snippet】\nL111: \nL112: Copy\nL113: \nL114:     ``\nL115: ## Citation\nL116: If you use Captar-Libras in your research, please cite it using the following BibTeX entry:\nL117: \nL118:     `@inproceedings{magalhaes2026captarlibras,\nL119:                     title={Captar-Libras: A Unified Multimodal Dataset for Brazilian Sign Language Tasks},\nL120:                     author={Cauã Magalhães and Bruno Lages and Ari Filho and Jéssica Ramos and Paulo de Souza Coelho and Michel Silva and Thiago L. Gomes and Milena Soriano Marcolino and Elidéa Bernardino and Raquel O. Prates and Mario F. M. Campos and Erickson R. Nascimento},\nL121:                     year={2026},\nL122:                     booktitle={The Fortieth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems Evaluations and Datasets Track}\nL123:                 }`\nL124: Copy\nL125: \nL126: © 2026 VeRLab - UFMG | Computer Vision and Robotics Lab\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLibras-UFPel Corpus: A Parallel Dataset of Brazilian Sign Language and Portuguese for Multimodal Research and Processing - ACL Anthology (https://aclanthology.org/2026.propor-1.112/)\nciteturn33view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2026.propor-1.112/\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 304\nL0:   * 【0†About】\nL1:     * 【1†Announcements】\nL2:     * 【2†Communication channels】\nL3:     * 【3†Related work】\nL4:     * 【4†Copyright】\nL5:     * * * *\nL6: \nL7:     * 【5†Credits】\nL8:     * 【6†Volunteer】\nL9:     * 【7†Development】\nL10:     * 【8†Feedback】\nL11:   * 【0†Using】\nL12:     * 【9†Author directory】\nL13:     * 【10†Citing papers】\nL14:     * 【11†Links in the Anthology】\nL15:     * 【12†Data access】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【13†All FAQs】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20: \nL21:     * ###### Details\nL22:     * 【14†Anthology identifiers】\nL23:     * 【15†Names】\nL24:     * 【16†ORCID iDs】\nL25:     * 【17†DOIs】\nL26:     * 【18†Verified authors】\nL27:   * 【0†Contributions】\nL28:     * 【19†Submissions】\nL29:     * 【20†Corrections】\nL30:     * 【21†Maintain author pages】\nL31:     * 【22†Attachments】\nL32: ## 【23†L ibras-UFP el Corpus: A Parallel Dataset of B razilian S ign L anguage and P ortuguese for Multimodal Research and Processing】\nL33: \nL34: 【24†Antonielle Martins】, 【25†Brenda S. Santana】, 【26†Francielle Martins】, 【27†Tatiana Lebedeff】, 【28†Darley Nunes】, 【29†Luisa Bohm】\nL35: ##### Correct Metadata for\nL36: \nL37: Use this form to create a GitHub issue with structured data describing the correction. You will need a GitHub account. Once you create that issue, the correction will be reviewed by a staff member.\nL38: \nL39: ⚠️ Mobile Users: Submitting this form to create a new issue will only work with github.com, not the GitHub Mobile app.\nL40: Important: The Anthology treat PDFs as authoritative. Please use this form only to correct data that is out of line with the PDF. See 【20†our corrections guidelines】 if you need to change the PDF.\nL41: \nL42: Title Adjust the title. Retain tags such as <fixed-case>. [Input]\nL43: \nL44: Authors Adjust author names and order to match the PDF.\nL45: \nL46: [Button: Add Author]\nL47: Abstract Correct abstract if needed. Retain XML formatting tags such as <tex-math>. You may use <b>...</b> for bold, <i>...</i> for italic, <u>...</u> for underline, <sc>...</sc> for small-caps, <tt>...<tt> for `typewriter text`, <url>...</url> for URLs, <a href=...> for hyperlinks, and <par/> for paragraph breaks.\nL48: \nL49: Verification against PDF Ensure that the new title/authors match the snapshot below. (If there is no snapshot or it is too small, consult 【0†the PDF】.)\nL50: Authors concatenated from the text boxes above:\nL51: \nL52: [Input] ALL author names match the snapshot above—including middle initials, hyphens, and accents.\nL53: \nL54: [Button: Create GitHub issue for staff review]\nL55: \nL56: * * *\nL57: ##### Abstract\nL58: The Libras-UFPel Corpus is a multimodal, multilayer parallel resource designed for the documentation and computational analysis of Brazilian Sign Language (Libras) in systematic alignment with written Portuguese. By integrating controlled recordings with naturalistic data from the Inventário Nacional de Libras-Pelotas, the corpus ensures interoperability through shared methodological standards.\nL59: The dataset currently comprises 4,800 controlledaudiovisual records (2,400 sentences and 2,400 isolated signs) fully paired with Portuguese translations, supplemented by approximately 10 hours of spontaneous interaction from threenew naturalistic interviews, currently in the editing phase.\nL60: To date, 1,200 controlled sentences have been lemmatized, gloss-annotatedand translated, providing a structured parallel subset for Libras-to-Portuguese Sign Language Processing tasks such as recognition and machine translation. The annotation model follows a hierarchical structure covering lexical, partially lexical, and non-lexical signs, including independent tiers for non-manual markers.\nL61: By bridging descriptive linguistics and Natural Language Processing, Libras-UFPel Corpus serves as a reference source for bilingual data-driven modeling, advancing digital inclusion and linguistic accessibility.\nL62: Anthology ID:\nL63:     2026.propor-1.112\nL64: Volume:\nL65:     【30†Proceedings of the 17th International Conference on Computational Processing of Portuguese (PROPOR 2026) - Vol. 1】\nL66: Month:\nL67:     April\nL68: Year:\nL69:     2026\nL70: Address:\nL71:     Salvador, Brazil\nL72: Editors:\nL73:     【31†Marlo Souza】, 【32†Iria de-Dios-Flores】, 【33†Diana Santos】, 【34†Larissa Freitas】, 【35†Jackson Wilke da Cruz Souza】, 【36†Eugénio Ribeiro】\nL74: Venue:\nL75:     【37†PROPOR】\nL76: SIG:\nL77: \nL78: Publisher:\nL79:     Association for Computational Linguistics\nL80: Note:\nL81: \nL82: Pages:\nL83:     1068–1073\nL84: Language:\nL85: \nL86: URL:\nL87:     【38†https://aclanthology.org/2026.propor-1.112/】\nL88: DOI:\nL89: \nL90: Bibkey:\nL91: \nL92: Cite (ACL):\nL93:     Antonielle Martins, Brenda S. Santana, Francielle Martins, Tatiana Lebedeff, Darley Nunes, and Luisa Bohm. 2026. 【38†Libras-UFPel Corpus: A Parallel Dataset of Brazilian Sign Language and Portuguese for Multimodal Research and Processing】. In Proceedings of the 17th International Conference on Computational Processing of Portuguese (PROPOR 2026) - Vol. 1, pages 1068–1073, Salvador, Brazil. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL94: Cite (Informal):\nL95:     【38†Libras-UFPel Corpus: A Parallel Dataset of Brazilian Sign Language and Portuguese for Multimodal R